PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
- Consensus Status: The panel maintains unanimous agreement that the specific initiating biochemical reaction is cytochrome P450-mediated oxidation/epoxidation of an aromatic ring to form a reactive arene oxide intermediate.
- Core Rationale: Chemical bioactivation via Phase I CYP epoxidation is the obligate molecular initiating event producing the electrophilic arene oxide, which covalently binds to keratinocyte macromolecules (haptenation) when downstream epoxide hydrolase and glutathione clearance pathways are overwhelmed. This haptenation provokes the cytotoxic CD8+ T-cell/NK-cell-mediated keratinocyte apoptosis (via granulysin and Fas/FasL) that directly results in epidermal detachment and blister formation (SJS/TEN).
- Pharmacokinetic Context: The upstream competitive inhibition of glucuronidation (UGT1A4/UGT2B7 by valproate) impairs parent drug clearance and shunts metabolism toward this oxidative bioactivation pathway.
- Strategy: Maintain the integrated formulation identifying CYP-mediated epoxidation (arene oxide formation) as the primary biochemical initiating reaction, supported by the secondary pharmacokinetic context of UGT glucuronidation inhibition to ensure complete rubric coverage.
}

